---
import { Code } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import Mark from './Mark.astro';
import { fidelity, fmt, issues, samples, url } from '../lib/data';

const f = fidelity;
const portableOff = f.e2e.corpora
	.map((c) => ({ name: c.name, n: c.total - c.portable }))
	.filter((c) => c.n > 0)
	.sort((a, b) => b.n - a.n);
const twopi = f.layout.find((l) => l.name === 'twopi')!;
const lastFuzz = f.fuzz.rounds[f.fuzz.rounds.length - 1];
const portBugs = issues.filter((i) => !i.upstream_c).length;
const upstream = issues.length - portBugs;
const live = samples.filter((s) => s.identical_portable).length;
const usage = `import init, { render } from '@graphvis-rs/wasm';

await init();
const svg = render('digraph { a -> b [label="fast"] }', 'dot', 'svg');`;
---

<div class="home">
	<section class="two">
		<div>
			<h2>What it is</h2>
			<p>
				A function-by-function port of the Graphviz 16.1.0 C sources: all eight layout engines (<code>dot</code>, <code>neato</code>,
				<code>fdp</code>, <code>sfdp</code>, <code>twopi</code>, <code>circo</code>, <code>osage</code>, <code>patchwork</code>), and the
				libraries under them: network simplex, <code>pathplan</code>, <code>ortho</code>, <code>pack</code>, <code>vpsc</code>, the
				shape, record and HTML-table code, and the output plugins.
			</p>
			<ul class="facts">
				<li><code>#![forbid(unsafe_code)]</code>; no C, no FFI, no system Graphviz.</li>
				<li>Runs natively and in the browser (<code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code>, 1.7&nbsp;MB, about 700&nbsp;KB compressed).</li>
				<li><code>dot-rs</code> takes the same flags as <code>dot</code> and exits with C's status.</li>
				<li>Writes <code>svg</code>, <code>json</code>, <code>json0</code>, <code>dot</code>, <code>xdot</code>, <code>canon</code>, <code>plain</code> and <code>plain-ext</code>.</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
		<div class="code">
			<Code code={usage} lang="js" title="Browser, Node, Deno, Bun or Workers" />
			<Code code={'cargo build --release -p gv-cli\ntarget/release/dot-rs -Kneato -Tsvg graph.gv'} lang="sh" title="Command line" />
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="define">
		<h2>What “identical” means here</h2>
		<div class="defgrid">
			<p>
				<strong>Equal bytes.</strong> The two outputs are compared after dropping one line, the <code>&lt;!-- Generated by … --&gt;</code>
				comment that names the program. Nothing else is normalised: not whitespace, not number formatting, not element order.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Against which C.</strong> The reference is Graphviz 16.1.0 with only its core, dot and neato plugins loaded, so no Pango plugin
				is available and C measures text with its built-in font metric tables, as the port does. A stock <code>dot</code> that measures text with Pango and cairo will place labels differently, and so
				will not match either build.{' '}<a href={url('verify/#the-c-reference')}>The setup</a>
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Which machine.</strong> C on macOS calls Apple's libm. The port reproduces it bit for bit on macOS, and uses a portable math
				library elsewhere, which is why Linux and WASM match on {fmt(f.e2e.portable)} files rather than {fmt(f.e2e.total)}.{' '}
				<a href={url('porting/libm/')}>Math off macOS</a>
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>What is not identical yet</h2>
		<ul class="left">
			<li>
				<Mark kind="off" />
				<span>
					<strong class="num">{fmt(f.e2e.total - f.e2e.portable)}</strong> files under portable math:{' '}
					{portableOff.map((c, i) => <>{i > 0 && ', '}{c.name} {c.n}</>)}. Apple's <code>pow</code>, <code>sin</code>, <code>cos</code> and
					<code>atan2</code> are closed source, and sfdp's multilevel layout magnifies their last-bit differences.
				</span>
			</li>
			<li>
				<Mark kind="upstream" />
				<span>
					<strong class="num">{twopi.total - twopi.apple}</strong> twopi layouts out of {twopi.total}: a floating-point tie in edge routing that C
					itself breaks differently from build to build. <strong class="num">{f.e2e.unstable.length}</strong> corpus files are skipped because
					C's own output changes between two runs.
				</span>
			</li>
			<li>
				<Mark kind="off" />
				<span>
					<strong class="num">{(100 - lastFuzz.all).toFixed(1)}%</strong> of {fmt(lastFuzz.cases)} random graphs in the last fuzzing round: mostly C
					nondeterminism and C crash paths, plus open differences.
				</span>
			</li>
			<li>
				<Mark kind="pending" />
				<span>
					<strong class="num">{issues.length}</strong> open issues: {portBugs} in the port, {upstream} upstream C behaviour.{' '}
					<a href={url('whats-left/')}>What's left</a>
				</span>
			</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Check it</h2>
		<ul class="next">
			<li><a href={url('fidelity/')}>Fidelity</a><span>Every check, per corpus and per math profile, with the files that differ.</span></li>
			<li><a href={url('gallery/')}>Gallery</a><span>{samples.length} inputs across all eight engines, C next to Rust, with overprint, blink, pixel and source diffs. {live} of them are re-rendered in your browser and compared with C's bytes.</span></li>
			<li><a href={url('playground/')}>Playground</a><span>Your own DOT, through the WASM build.</span></li>
			<li><a href={url('verify/')}>Verify it yourself</a><span>The commands, the C reference setup, and where the goldens live.</span></li>
			<li><a href={url('porting/dotgen/')}>Porting notes</a><span>Each Rust module mapped to its C file, and every known difference.</span></li>
			<li><a href={url('api/')}>API</a><span>rustdoc for the workspace crates.</span></li>
		</ul>
	</section>
</div>

<style>
	.home {
		margin-top: 1rem;
	}
	.home h2 {
		font-size: var(--sl-text-xl);
		font-weight: 700;
		font-stretch: 112%;
		margin: 3rem 0 1rem;
		color: var(--key);
	}
	.two {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
		gap: 2.5rem;
		align-items: start;
	}
	.two .code {
		margin-top: 3rem;
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		gap: 1rem;
	}
	.two .code > :global(*) {
		margin: 0 !important;
	}
	.facts {
		padding-left: 1.125rem;
		color: var(--key-2);
	}
	.facts li + li {
		margin-top: 0.25rem;
	}
	.defgrid {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
		gap: 1.5rem;
		border-top: 0.5px solid var(--rule-strong);
		padding-top: 1rem;
	}
	.defgrid p {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
		line-height: 1.6;
		color: var(--key-2);
	}
	.defgrid strong {
		display: block;
		color: var(--key);
		font-weight: 650;
		margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
	}
	.left {
		list-style: none;
		padding: 0;
		margin: 0;
		display: grid;
		gap: 0;
		border-top: 0.5px solid var(--rule-strong);
	}
	.left li {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1.5rem 1fr;
		gap: 0.5rem;
		padding: 0.75rem 0;
		margin: 0;
		border-bottom: 0.5px solid var(--rule);
		color: var(--key-2);
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
	}
	.left li :global(.mark) {
		padding-top: 0.2rem;
	}
	.left strong {
		color: var(--key);
		font-weight: 650;
	}
	.next {
		list-style: none;
		padding: 0;
		margin: 0;
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
		column-gap: 2.5rem;
		border-top: 0.5px solid var(--rule-strong);
	}
	.next li {
		display: grid;
		gap: 0.125rem;
		padding: 0.75rem 0;
		margin: 0;
		border-bottom: 0.5px solid var(--rule);
	}
	.next a {
		font-weight: 650;
		font-stretch: 112%;
		color: var(--register);
		text-decoration: none;
	}
	.next a:hover {
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
	}
	.next span {
		font-size: var(--sl-text-sm);
		color: var(--key-3);
	}
	.home a {
		color: var(--register);
	}
	@media (max-width: 50rem) {
		.two,
		.defgrid,
		.next {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		}
		.two .code {
			margin-top: 0;
		}
	}
</style>
